		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=41862#p41862"><![CDATA[
No one respond .. nice. The issue is in fact still open on github (<a href="https://github.com/bulletphysics/bullet3/issues/2020" class="postlink">https://github.com/bulletphysics/bullet3/issues/2020</a>) and erwin coumans ignore the <span style="text-decoration:underline"><strong class="text-strong">BUG</strong></span> still 1 month after. The only way i found is complexity O(n^4) which it's very very bad conpare to the real who is O(n^2)<br><br>Solution for intrested person who need to have a proof of concept : <br><div class="codebox"><p>Code: </p><pre><code>for i in range(-1, nbobj):        for j in range(-1, nbobj):            for elem1 in range(0, nbPop):                for elem2 in range(elem1+1, nbPop):                      p.setCollisionFilterPair(pop[elem1].getId(), pop[elem2].getId(), i, j, 0)        print((i+2)/(nbobj+1))</code></pre></div> <br><br>We need to remove the collision pair by pair so if you have 100 objects it's a 100*100 calculation. the fun doesn't end here.... if you have 10 piece in your multibody you need still to remove the collision pair by pair AGAIN. So it's actualy 100*100*10*10 calculation...<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=13209">Lewho</a> — Tue Feb 12, 2019 2:10 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
